• I’ll inspect the existing judgments, scanners, and proof patterns, then add the four pieces without introducing any undecided token rules.

• The existing code uses structural recursion and independent inductive judgments. I’ll follow that pattern, with the string scanner parameterized by a decidable raw-scalar predicate and longest match defined only over an arbitrary table.

• I’ll add the specification definitions first: maximal identifier and digit runs, escape-sensitive string bodies, and a table-parametric longest-match judgment.

• The four Surface0 judgments elaborate. Next I’m adding the scanners and proofs piece by piece so each correspondence theorem is checked against the actual implementation.

• The single-file check needs the edited specification built first; I’ll build that module, then rerun the lexer elaboration.

• Identifier scanning now proves sound and complete; only the uniqueness wrapper needed option-injection rather than the pair-rewriting used for trivia. I’m correcting that and adding string-body scanning next.

• String escapes exposed two proof issues: the prepend lemma’s output type was too broad, and the two-scalar escape step needs the scanner’s generated induction principle rather than ordinary one-step list induction.

• The generated string induction distinguishes the five excluded head shapes plus the raw predicate. I’m naming those cases explicitly and fixing `rawOk` so the proof cannot accidentally generalize the parameter.

• Identifier and string-body scanning now elaborate with their uniqueness theorems. I’m adding longest match over an abstract table, including the no-match case and proof that equal-length longest results are identical.

• The first longest-match proof pass found only Lean-shape issues, plus one real gap in the no-match branch: empty table entries must be handled by length, not treated as contradictions. I’m correcting both before moving on.

• I’ve isolated the remaining longest-match corrections to constructor patterns, match reduction, and the empty-entry length case; the declarative definition itself is unchanged.

• The longest-match soundness proof is now down to simplifying matches that `cases` has already rewritten. After that, only the decimal scanner remains.

• Longest match now proves soundness, completeness, and uniqueness for both matches and `none`. I’m adding the maximal digit scanner with the exact `0`-alone leading-zero rule and arbitrary-precision `Nat` values.

• The decimal rules are in place; the remaining errors are dependent-pair reductions and one constructor-pattern name, not changes to the lexeme semantics.

• All scanner proofs now pass except the single `0` case, where Lean is retaining unreduced pair projections in the acceptance goal. I’ll make that constructor’s digit list and value explicit.

• All four scanners and their correspondence proofs now elaborate without warnings. I’m adding kernel-checked accepted/refused cases, including all six escapes and the required integer examples.

• The conformance cases all pass, including refused bad escapes, raw LF/CR, EOF, `007`, and `00`. Next I’ll regenerate and audit the axiom inventory, run its verification gate, and build the full default target.

• The axiom gate passes. Its golden diff has only 15 added lines for the new `DigitRun`, `IdentBody`, `StringBody`, and `DigitsAccepted` declarations; each is axiom-free or uses only `propext`, and no existing line changed.

• `lake build` and `lake build Concrete` both succeed. I’m doing the final diff review now; the only `git diff --check` reports are the generated golden file’s normal tab separators, so I’ll check the handwritten files separately.

• The final review found only the four requested files: the two Lean modules, the regenerated golden, and the changelog. No undecided tables, suffix rules, diagnostics, or banned proof constructs were added.

• Implemented and committed as `da687c9ec9bef1a5fd301564a7afef4104065687` on `wip/slice0-lexer-tokens`. The commit records the dependency on unlanded `e474d8f0`. Nothing was pushed; the worktree is clean.

